@database "file_checkpoints"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: file_checkpoints.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "File checkpoints"
@{h1}File checkpoints

Checkpoints for files, not only for the conversation. @{tt}/undo@{utt}
takes the last exchange out of the chat but leaves the files the agent wrote
in it as they are. With this plugin the agent's file changes are recorded
turn by turn -- how each file looked before the agent first touched it -- so
the chat can put them back: @{tt}/undo files@{utt} drops the last exchange
together with its file changes, @{tt}/rewind@{utt} lists the checkpoints of
a session and @{tt}/rewind <n>@{utt} puts the files back as they were before
checkpoint n. A rewind never touches a file the agent did not change, and
refuses when a file was changed by someone else since. The model gets no
tool: undoing its work is the person's call. It has no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Undo and rewind in the chat  " link using}
  @{"  What a rewind does and refuses  " link rewind}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What is recorded  " link recorded}
  @{"  The two hooks  " link hooks}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node using "Undo and rewind in the chat"
@{h1}Undo and rewind in the chat

The same words work in the terminal chat (@{tt}agent-cli@{utt}) and in the
browser chat. They only have something to rewind when the agent of the
session records checkpoints (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}); the coder
agent and its sub-agents do.

@{table}
Command | Does
/undo files | puts back every file the last exchange changed, then drops the exchange
/retry files | the same, then asks the question again
/rewind | lists the checkpoints of the session
/rewind <n> | puts the files back as they were before checkpoint n; the conversation stays as it is
/undo files overwrite | also puts back files changed outside the agent since
/rewind <n> overwrite | the same for a checkpoint
@{body}

A word may carry leading dashes (@{tt}/undo --files@{utt}).
@{tt}overwrite@{utt} goes only with @{tt}files@{utt} or with a checkpoint
number; @{tt}/rewind overwrite@{utt} alone is refused with the usage line.
In the browser, @{tt}force@{utt} takes over a session a crashed process
still holds, as with plain @{tt}/undo@{utt}.

@{h2}The list

@{code}
File checkpoints -- /rewind <n> puts the files back as they were before checkpoint n:
   3  turn 2: "add a config loader"
      2 file(s): created src/config.py, modified src/main.py
   5  dropped turn (after turn 2): "try it with yaml"
      1 file(s): modified src/config.py
    Not tracked: coder_shell_execute x2 -- whatever they changed is still as they left it.
@{body}

One entry per turn that changed files, oldest first, with the question that
opened it and up to five of its files. The number is the record's own and
never shifts: when an old checkpoint is forgotten, @{tt}/rewind 5@{utt}
still means the same one -- so numbers have gaps. A "dropped turn" is one
@{tt}/undo@{utt} took out of the conversation without its files; its changes
are still on disk, and a rewind to a turn before it undoes them too.

@{h2}What the answer says

A rewind answers with one of four results:

@{table}
Result | Meaning
rewound | every recorded change since the checkpoint is undone; "put back" and "removed" name the files
nothing | no file needed putting back
refused | nothing was touched; the files that stopped it are named with the reason
partial | the rest was put back; the named files were not, and stay recorded -- rewind again once the reason is gone
@{body}

A number that names no checkpoint answers with the numbers there are.

@{tt}/undo files@{utt} drops the exchange only after "rewound" or
"nothing". After "refused" or "partial" the exchange stays, so you can look
and try again; a plain @{tt}/undo@{utt} drops it and leaves the files (their
record stays for a later @{tt}/rewind@{utt}).

Tools that change files without naming them -- shell commands and the like
-- are not recorded, only counted: the answer ends with "Not tracked:
<tool> xN", so a "rewound" is never mistaken for "exactly as before".

In the browser the answers come from the server: a refusal is a 409, an
unknown checkpoint a 404, a partial rewind a 500 with the same text, and
503 when the plugin is not loaded ("File checkpoints are off"). A session
whose run is still going is refused with 409 -- the run could write while
files are put back.
@endnode

@node rewind "What a rewind does and refuses"
@{h1}What a rewind does and refuses

For every path changed since the checkpoint, the target is the state before
the agent's first change, and the plugin knows the state the agent's last
change left. It compares both with the disk before it writes anything.

@{h2}Refused -- nothing is touched

@{bullet}
@{b}Changed outside the agent.@{ub} The file is not what the agent left
(someone edited it since), or it changed between two of the agent's calls --
in one turn or between turns. The path is named; @{tt}overwrite@{utt} puts
it back anyway.
@{b}Cannot be put back.@{ub} Its content was never kept (larger than
@{tt}max_file_bytes@{utt}, the store was full, it could not be read); a
directory beyond @{tt}max_entries_per_call@{utt}; the tool server that
recorded it is not loaded or no longer allows the path; a link now stands in
its path; a directory that holds files the agent did not create. With
@{tt}overwrite@{utt} the rest is put back and these are named.
@{b}A run is going.@{ub} A run of the session, or a sub-agent it started,
is still working.
@{body}

@{h2}What it never does

@{bullet}
It never deletes a path it has no record of. A directory the agent created
is removed only when everything in it goes with it; otherwise it is kept,
named, and stays recorded until you clear it.
It never writes outside the directories of the tool server that recorded a
path. Paths are recorded as that server's own sandbox resolved them, and
every write is checked again -- on its resolved parent directory, on the
disk as it is at that moment. A link the rewind itself puts back higher up
cannot carry a write out.
It never writes through a link: a file is replaced by a rename, a link is
recreated as a link, a delete removes the link, not its target.
It never overwrites a file that changed while the rewind ran: each path is
looked at again right before it is written and left as it is if it moved.
@{body}

@{h2}What stays recorded

Paths that could not be written (no permission, a file held open by another
program on Windows) and paths refused for a reason that may pass (server not
loaded, a link in the path, your files in a directory) stay recorded: the
answer is "partial", and a later rewind finishes them. What can never come
back (content not kept) is forgotten with the rest. After a rewind the turns
it undid are forgotten; a files-only @{tt}/rewind@{utt} leaves them in the
conversation.

@{h2}Two sessions, one file

Each session has its own record. If two sessions change the same file, a
rewind in the first finds the file is not what its agent left and refuses;
@{tt}overwrite@{utt} would put back the first session's older state over the
second's work.
@endnode

@node recorded "What is recorded"
@{h1}What is recorded

@{table}
Tool (server type) | Recorded
file_ops manage create, delete, move, rename; replace_string_in_file | the state before: content and mode, a link's target, a directory, or "did not exist"; every instance, whatever its name
media_ops save | the target file, the same way
terminal execute, coding_cli run_task, blender, godot, audio_ops, image_compose, comfyui, sqlite_query, stategraph machine tools, and untracked_tools | not recorded, counted per turn
everything else | nothing
@{body}

@{bullet}
@{b}Once per turn and path.@{ub} A file edited twenty times in a turn costs
one copy; a file nobody touches costs nothing. Identical content is stored
once (by its SHA-256).
@{b}Directories.@{ub} A recursive delete, a move or a rename of a directory
records every entry under it (links not followed), up to
@{tt}max_entries_per_call@{utt}; beyond that, the directory is recorded as
one whose contents were not kept. Directories a create makes on its way are
recorded as new.
@{b}The path the tool writes.@{ub} Paths come from the server's own sandbox
(file_ops' validator, media_ops' sandbox). A path the sandbox refuses is not
recorded -- the tool refuses it too. A read-only instance records nothing.
@{b}One file, one record.@{ub} On a case-insensitive file system
@{tt}Foo.txt@{utt} and @{tt}foo.txt@{utt} are one file: paths are recorded
under the spelling the directory stores, and a file comes back under the
name it had.
@{b}Calls that changed nothing@{ub} (a replace that did not match) are
dropped again after the call.
@{body}

@{h2}Turns

A change belongs to the turn that was the conversation's last one when it
was recorded -- the message @{tt}/undo@{utt} cuts at. Turns are identified
by their first message (role, run id, timestamp), and each keeps the keys of
the turns before it. That places every record in the conversation as it is
now, even after @{tt}/undo@{utt}, @{tt}/retry@{utt} or compaction.

@{h2}Sub-agents and scripts

A run started under a recorded run records into that run's session and turn
-- if its agent has the hooks on too. "Started under" means its request id
extends the run's by what the framework appends (@{tt}_NNN@{utt} tool call,
@{tt}_tsNN@{utt} script call, @{tt}_sub_@{utt} or @{tt}_async_@{utt}
sub-agent) and its session is the run's own or a sub-session of it. A
sub-agent without the hooks records nothing; the call that started it is
counted like a shell command ("... (agent 'x' records no checkpoints)").
tool_script calls pass the same hooks and are recorded like the model's.

@{h2}Not recorded

@{bullet}
Throwaway sessions (@{tt}ephemeral-...@{utt}: openai_api without
@{tt}store@{utt}, a stateless collect_final_result) and the runs they start.
Runs without a user or without a session id a chat could name.
Anything a shell command, a machine activity, a slash command or a web
button writes -- these pass no file hook.
@{body}
@endnode

@node hooks "The two hooks"
@{h1}The two hooks

@{table}
Hook | Type | Does
record_before_change | pre_tool_call | before a call that changes named files: records each path's current state; counts calls that change files without naming them
record_after_change | post_tool_call | after such a call: records what it left, or drops the row when the call changed nothing
@{body}

Both are off for every agent. An agent switches @{b}both@{ub} on in its YAML,
with the full names:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      file_checkpoints.record_before_change: {enabled: true}
      file_checkpoints.record_after_change: {enabled: true}
@{body}

One without the other records nothing and logs an error once per agent:
what a call left is how a rewind tells the agent's change from one made
outside it. There are no other per-agent settings -- every agent of a
session writes into the same record; the limits are the instance's (see
@{" Setting it up " link setup}).

@{tt}record_before_change@{utt} runs after the hooks that change arguments
and after tool_approval: a call blocked there is not recorded, and the
arguments recorded are the ones the call runs with. Its timeout is 60
seconds (a recursive delete of a big tree is the slow case). Neither hook
changes the call or its result, and neither blocks it: a record that cannot
be written is logged and the call runs unrecorded.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

Nothing. There is no tool, no injected message and no status line; both
hooks pass the call and its result through unchanged. Nothing reaches the
prompt or the history, so there is no token cost and no effect on the
prompt cache.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}
Shell commands and other tools that write without naming the file are only
counted. Recording them would need a snapshot of the whole tree around every
call.
A sub-agent without the hooks records nothing; the call that started it is
named instead.
Stategraph machines call their tools without hooks: their runs are counted,
their file changes are not recorded.
Slash commands and web buttons that write files pass no tool hook.
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The instance in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
    file_checkpoints:
      type: file_checkpoints
      enabled: true
      config:
        max_checkpoints: 50
        retention_days: 0
@{body}

That loads it and makes the chat commands work; nothing is recorded until
an agent switches the two hooks on (see @{" The two hooks " link hooks}). The
coder agent and its sub-agents coder_explorer, coder_reviewer and
coder_tester do. There is no tool, so nothing goes in the allowlist. No
extra packages.

@{h2}Settings

Under @{tt}config:@{utt} in the instance entry, read once when the plugin
starts.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
storage_path | "" | where the records live; empty: a directory file_checkpoints beside the session directory; relative paths count from the installation
max_checkpoints | 50 | turns with recorded changes kept per session (at least 1); the oldest is forgotten first
max_file_bytes | 10485760 | a file larger than this is recorded but not copied; a rewind names it
max_call_bytes | 268435456 | content copied for one call at most; the rest is recorded without content
max_session_bytes | 536870912 | content kept per session; older turns are forgotten to make room, the turn being recorded never
max_entries_per_call | 2000 | a directory with more entries is recorded as one, its contents not kept
retention_days | 0 | records untouched this many days are deleted, at start and once a day while recording; 0: never
untracked_tools | [] | more "server_type/tool" patterns (fnmatch) counted like shell commands
@{body}

Forgetting old turns only shortens how far back a rewind reaches: it deletes
copies this plugin made, never your files. Deleting whole records by age is
the operator's decision, hence off by default.

@{h2}Where the data lives

@{tt}data/file_checkpoints/<user>/<session>/@{utt} -- with
@{tt}AGENT_SESSION_STORAGE_PATH@{utt} set, next to those sessions instead.
Each holds @{tt}journal.db@{utt} (SQLite: turns, changes, counted calls) and
@{tt}blobs/<sha256>@{utt} (the replaced bytes, each once). The user is part
of the address and the journal names its owner: a rewind only reads the
asking user's record, and the chat endpoints check that the session is
theirs first. Deleting a session (@{tt}DELETE /api/sessions/<id>@{utt}) takes
its record along; archiving does not, so a restored session keeps its
checkpoints.
@endnode
